Then the useful object to model is not “content” but the influence field around it.

Think of every post, comment, reaction, remix, share, recommendation, silence, and imitation as a directional edge carrying some mixture of attention, emotion, information, status, and behavioral pressure. The network is continuously updating itself:

S_t
\rightarrow
\{\text{exposures, reactions, imitation, rejection, remix}\}
\rightarrow
S_{t+1}

A compact way to break it apart is:

* Vertical influence: creator → audience, institution → public, expert → learner.
* Reverse influence: audience → creator through comments, metrics, purchases, ridicule, requests, and norms.
* Lateral influence: viewer ↔ viewer, peer ↔ peer, community ↔ community.
* Algorithmic influence: platform ranking determines which signals become disproportionately observable.
* Reflexive influence: people change behavior because they know they are being watched or measured.
* Second-order influence: people respond not to the original message, but to how they think everyone else is interpreting it.
* Cross-network leakage: a pattern jumps platforms, communities, languages, or media forms and mutates during translation.

That yields a much richer object:

I_{ij}(t,m,c)

where i and j are actors, t is time, m is the modality or message, and c is context. Crucially,

I_{ij}\neq I_{ji}

and influence doesn’t necessarily imply agreement. Mockery, resistance, fascination, imitation, and avoidance can all propagate something.

Then add your dye idea. Introduce harmless distinguishable variants and observe:

\text{origin}
\rightarrow
\text{path}
\rightarrow
\text{mutation}
\rightarrow
\text{reinforcement}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}

Now you can estimate the latent topology underneath what appears on the screen. Two communities may look disconnected yet repeatedly reproduce the same distinctive mutation; that suggests some hidden bridge, common source, or convergent pressure worth investigating.

And then apply the sensory trick: temporarily remove likes, comments, usernames, audio, captions, chronology, or recommendation context. See what interpretation survives.

That lets you separate:

\text{intrinsic signal}

from

\text{socially supplied interpretation}

from

\text{algorithmically supplied interpretation}.

The deeper Pantheon operator here could be Influence Tomography: reconstruct the hidden social system by observing how known signals propagate through it from multiple directions, scales, and sensory views.

Not “who controls whom,” but:

What is coupled to what, through which channels, with what delay, transformation, gain, and feedback?

Once you phrase it that way, social media starts looking much less like a collection of people watching videos and much more like a living dynamical system you can actually study.